# A daily backup of the Kinige server with the rotation (spike S4c-2, ADR-0020). Put this in /etc/cron.d/kinige-backup after changing the
# path (the /etc/cron.d format has a user field between the weekday and the command; a per-user crontab does not). It takes a snapshot of
# Postgres, copies the blobs, writes the manifest and removes backups older than KINIGE_BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS (30 by default).
#
# The newest backup is kept whatever its age, until the next one is made: a server whose cron stopped keeps its last backup instead of
# none. So a backup that holds a deleted account's data lives until the next backup replaces it as the newest and the retention has
# passed, not exactly 30 days; to hold the hard bound, keep this entry running (ADR-0001 §8.9, PRD §10.3).
#
# The interval between runs must be what KINIGE_BACKUP_INTERVAL_HOURS says (24 by default): a deleted blob stays in the store for the
# retention plus one interval, which is how long the oldest backup that can still be restored lives.
#
# Copy the backups volume off this machine as well (rsync, restic, a snapshot of the volume): a backup on the disk it protects is
# not one.
17 3 * * * root cd /opt/kinige/deploy && docker compose --profile tools run --rm -T backup >> /var/log/kinige-backup.log 2>&1
